---
import TrainingLayout from "../../layouts/TrainingLayout.astro";
import Snippet from "../../components/training/Snippet.astro";
import LiveDemo from "../../components/training/LiveDemo.astro";
import ChapterLink from "../../components/training/ChapterLink.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/training/Callout.astro";
---
<TrainingLayout slug="why-big-tables-freeze">

  <h2 id="problem">The problem</h2>
  <p>
    You have to show orders, measurements, log lines, positions – and there are many of them. The obvious solution is a
    plain HTML table: one <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code> per record, one <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> per value. It is simple, it is
    accessible, and for a few hundred rows it is perfect.
  </p>
  <p>Here it is, written in the most straightforward way – the code from the first video:</p>
  <Snippet file="demos/naive-table.ts" region="render" title="naive-table.ts" />

  <h2 id="try">Try it</h2>
  <p>
    Start the demo with 1,000 rows, then try 5,000 and 20,000. Click a column header to sort. Watch the numbers in the
    toolbar – and how long the page does not react at all.
  </p>
  <LiveDemo name="naive-table" title="The naive table" height={380}
            caption="20,000 rows block this page for several seconds. The step-by-step demo goes up to 100,000 rows – that takes minutes." />

  <h2 id="numbers">The numbers</h2>
  <p>Measured in Chromium (headless, Linux VM, October 2026) until the browser has really painted:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th class="num">Rows</th><th class="num">DOM elements</th><th class="num">First render</th><th class="num">Sort (rebuild everything)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="num">1,000</td><td class="num">12,014</td><td class="num">0.2 s</td><td class="num">0.2 s</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="num">10,000</td><td class="num">120,014</td><td class="num">2.7 s</td><td class="num">2.9 s</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="num">25,000</td><td class="num">300,014</td><td class="num">11.7 s</td><td class="num">11.9 s</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="num">50,000</td><td class="num">600,014</td><td class="num">41.5 s</td><td class="num">44.4 s</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="num">100,000</td><td class="num">1,200,014</td><td class="num">&gt; 2 min (browser blocked)</td><td class="num">–</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Twice the rows does <strong>not</strong> mean twice the time: from 25,000 to 50,000 rows the render time grows by a
    factor of 3.5. Layout, style calculation and memory grow faster than the number of rows. And sorting is just as
    expensive as the first render, because every cell has to be built again:
  </p>
  <Snippet file="demos/naive-table.ts" region="sort" />

  <h2 id="more">And that is only displaying</h2>
  <p>A real data grid needs much more than drawing rows. All of this is still missing:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>fixed header and fixed columns while scrolling – <ChapterLink slug="fixed-columns" /></li>
    <li>column widths, resizing, moving columns – <ChapterLink slug="table-options" /></li>
    <li>sorting with own rules and filtering – <ChapterLink slug="sorting" />, <ChapterLink slug="filtering" /></li>
    <li>selection with mouse and keyboard, copy to the clipboard – <ChapterLink slug="selection-and-copy" /></li>
    <li>Excel export – <ChapterLink slug="excel-export" /></li>
    <li>tree structures, merged cells, inline editing, live updates – parts 6 and 7</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout type="note" title="Pagination is not the answer">
    <p>Splitting the data into pages of 50 rows hides the problem, but it changes the product: users can no longer scroll,
      search with the browser, select across pages or compare row 12 with row 4,812. The rest of this training shows how to
      keep <em>all</em> rows in one scrollable table.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="next">Next</h2>
  <p>
    The way out is a simple idea: draw only what is visible. In <ChapterLink slug="virtual-scrolling" /> you build it
    yourself in about 20 lines – and see why you still want a library for it.
  </p>
</TrainingLayout>
